 "note": [
  "Taylor quotes scripture constantly and almost never cites it, so the references were identified by the chapter translators (seeded from the fr/sw editions' notes, then extended by reading). Every quotation uses the Bíblia Livre para o Mundo (porbrbsl, ebible mirror por_bpm): where it says what Taylor quotes, the words were pasted verbatim and the row is `mined` (109 rows, each re-verified by script as a substring of the porbrbsl verse); where Taylor re-persons, abridges, blends or alludes, the row is `self_rendered` in porbrbsl's register (61 rows) and needs a native check.",
  "Inner quotation marks that porbrbsl prints inside a verse were dropped where the English has none (Psalm 91:2, Proverbs 24:12), to keep the source's quote marks; Isaiah 53:3 keeps porbrbsl's “familiarizado com a enfermidade” verbatim, a shade from the KJV's “grief”. Acts 9:6 (“Senhor, que queres que eu faça?”) is the KJV/Textus Receptus wording, absent from porbrbsl, so it is self-rendered.",
  "Title “Uma Retrospectiva” matches the shipped pt study guide (a-retrospect-guide.pt) and the pt Hudson Taylor bio; the young-reader editions dropped the article only for the 28-character rail-cover limit. Sentences the guide already quotes from the book were used verbatim, and chapter 17 takes the guide's title “Deus, um Refúgio para Nós”. Matthew 5:42 uses porbrbsl (“Dê a quem lhe pedir”) rather than the guide's paraphrase.",
  "Place names follow the shipped pt corpus: Xangai, Cantão, Nanquim; Ningpo and Swatow (as in a-retrospect-children/teens.pt) and Taylor's other period romanisations kept. Money stays British (meia-coroa, xelins, pence, libras); the Chinese cash coin is “caixa”. Divine pronouns lowercase as in a-retrospect-teens.pt; reader address você; tu/vós only in Scripture and prayer."
 ],
